# Project OS — private files to keep out of git in a project that uses it.
#
# This is the curated list the installer merges into a target project's
# .gitignore. It is DELIBERATELY not the repo's own .gitignore: that file also
# ignores lines specific to THIS repository (a bare `app`, `code-graph.json`,
# `arachne-out/`, `command-center/`, `CLAUDE.md.pre-symlink.bak`, `memory/*.json`),
# and copying it wholesale silently un-tracked, e.g., a user's own `app/`
# directory (audit 2026-07-25). Only patterns that are correct for ANY project
# belong here.
#
# ANCHORING: a pattern with a slash is anchored to the repo root; the code can
# write these paths at any depth, so those rules use a leading `**/`.

# Private memory, vector stores, and the shared brain — never commit these
private-memory/
private-imports/
local-memory/
vector-index/
vector-store/
**/memory/store/
memory/store/
# The retrieval index is derived from the CENTRAL brain, not this project's own.
# `mneme_adapter.py` ships into every install and stores `text[:240]` of each
# lesson verbatim, so a lesson harvested while working on one client ends up in
# plaintext inside the next client's repository, ready for `git add -A`. The
# repo's own .gitignore has always covered this via `memory/*.json`, but that
# rule was dropped from this curated list as repository-specific — it is not
# (audit 2026-07-26). Named explicitly rather than restoring `memory/*.json`,
# which would silently untrack a user's own files under memory/.
**/mneme_index.json
.turbovec/
knowledge-graph/
graphify-out/
**/shared-brain.jsonl
brain/shared-brain.jsonl
# scripts/brain_archive.py moves records OUT of the brain into a sibling
# `-archive.jsonl` and leaves a `.pre-archive-<stamp>` copy of the original
# next to it. Both hold the same lesson text as the file above, and neither
# was covered by it (audit 2026-07-26).
**/shared-brain-archive.jsonl
**/shared-brain.jsonl.pre-archive-*
**/*.sidecar.json
**/store/*.tvim
central-brain/
**/central-brain/
.project-os-central-brain/
private-screenshots/
private-exports/
raw-exports/

# Secrets
.env
.env.*
.envrc
secret.env
*.key
*.pem
*.p12
*.pfx
id_rsa
id_ed25519
secrets/
secrets
.secrets
secrets.*

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
venv/

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/

# Agent / MCP tooling state — per-machine caches, never ship
.serena/
.aider*
.claude/settings.local.json

# Build / output scratch
tmp/
temp/
dist/
build/
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
*.db-journal
# brain_fts_mirror.py builds the full-text mirror in a `*.db.tmp` scratch file
# and only then os.replace()s it into place. Its cleanup is in a `finally`, so
# a Python-level error is handled — but a SIGKILL, an OOM or a power cut leaves
# the FULLY POPULATED scratch file behind, holding brain records in plaintext.
# The installer already refuses to COPY this family; leaving it out of the list
# it WRITES made it untracked-but-not-ignored in a stranger's project, one
# `git add -A` from being committed (adversarial pre-push audit 2026-07-26).
*.db.tmp
*.db.tmp-*
*.log
*.tvim
node_modules/
# `memory/code_graph.py build` writes its graph to <--root>/code-graph.json by
# default, and --root defaults to the project root — so the shipped tool drops a
# generated file into a stranger's repo that nothing ignores (audit 2026-07-26).
# Unlike `mneme_index.json` this is NOT a cross-project data leak: the graph is
# derived from the user's OWN source and holds nothing they don't already track.
# It is regenerable build noise, ignored for the same reason `dist/` is.
# Unanchored on purpose: --root can point at any directory, at any depth.
code-graph.json

# Project OS run working directories.
# `runs/` and `outputs/` hold transient per-run working state and can get large;
# they are ignored by default. `blackboard/` is your project's human-readable
# source of truth — it is NOT ignored here, so you can commit it if you want it
# in history. Delete the next two lines if you would rather track runs/outputs.
runs/
outputs/
